Chapter 7: The Human Agency Preservation Index

7.1 The Multiplicative Form and Its Justification

We propose a multiplicative, non-substitutable metric:

HAI = A \times C \times P \times R

where:

· $A$ = individual agency
· $C$ = contestability
· $P$ = participation
· $R$ = reversibility

The multiplicative form is justified by a non-substitutability argument drawing on the capability approach and rights-based theories of democratic legitimacy. A system that provides high agency but no contestability (e.g., a benevolent dictator) has a fundamentally different normative status than one that provides both. The multiplicative form encodes this non-substitutability: if any dimension is zero, the overall index is zero.

This is a strong normative position. The analysis defends it as follows: democratic legitimacy is not a matter of averaging across dimensions but of satisfying minimum thresholds on multiple dimensions simultaneously. A system that scores perfectly on three dimensions but fails entirely on the fourth is not three-quarters legitimate; it is illegitimate. The multiplicative form captures this threshold structure.

7.2 The Relationship Between AI Success and Human Agency

Consequently:

\boxed{\text{AI Success} = \text{Capability} \times \text{Human Agency}}

A highly capable AI that destroys human agency has zero social value. This formulation operationalizes the principle of Human-Machine Complementarity articulated in the Digital Statecraft Framework: computational capability is valuable only insofar as it serves human flourishing.

7.3 Empirical Limitations

The HAI is presented as a theoretical index framework requiring future empirical calibration. The operationalization of each dimension ($A, C, P, R$) requires:

· Specification of measurement indicators
· Validation of the multiplicative structure
· Calibration of the zero points
· Testing of the non-substitutability assumption

These tasks constitute a post-doctoral research program, not a doctoral thesis deliverable.

7.4 Normative Foundations

The non-substitutability assumption and the multiplicative form require engagement with:

· Pluralist theories of legitimacy (which allow trade-offs across dimensions)
· Consequentialist arguments (which might accept agency reduction for aggregate welfare gains)
· Non-ideal theory (which may prioritize contestability over participation in minimally democratic contexts)

This analysis adopts the position that democratic legitimacy requires simultaneous satisfaction of minimum thresholds across all four dimensions. This is defended by appeal to the intrinsic value of democratic processes—not merely their instrumental value for producing good outcomes. The defense proceeds through a rights-based argument: individuals have claims to agency, contestability, participation, and reversibility that cannot be traded off against aggregate welfare gains.

