10.5 Process Tracing Findings

Case 1: European Cloud Dependency. Process tracing reveals a pattern of progressive functional sovereignty transfer: initial procurement decisions driven by cost efficiency create architectural lock-in (Type 3 dependency), which subsequently produces epistemic dependency (Type 2) as public sector technical talent migrates to hyperscalers. The GAIA-X initiative failed to address the underlying political economy: without mandatory capacity reservations and unbundled API access, voluntary frameworks cannot reverse lock-in dynamics.

Case 2: China's Social Credit System. The Chinese case illustrates the endpoint of executive-bureaucratic capture. Algorithmic governance systems are deployed through state-owned enterprises, eliminating private sector dependency but substituting political dependency on the executive. The system demonstrates that compute concentration alone is not the problem; the alignment of computational capability with executive authority produces the Artificial State. This validates the proposition that $A_{HD} \to 0$ when $A_{HE}$ and $A_{HB}$ are aligned with computational capability.

Case 3: Global South Sovereignty. Selected African and Latin American cases reveal a distinct failure mode: infrastructure dependency on foreign cloud providers creates not only operational dependency (Type 1) but also diplomatic vulnerability. The ability of foreign states to restrict cloud access creates a new form of computational sovereignty violation that traditional sovereignty frameworks fail to address.

