Appendix B: The Democratic Compute Concentration Risk—Formal Decomposition

B.1 Hardware Concentration Index

H_{\text{Hardware}}(t) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} s_i^2(t)

where $s_i(t)$ is the share of strategically relevant AI compute (measured in effective FLOPs) controlled by actor $i$ at time $t$. This is the standard Herfindahl-Hirschman Index applied to compute infrastructure.

Properties:

1. Range: $H_{\text{Hardware}} \in [1/N, 1]$, where $N$ is the number of actors.
2. Perfect competition: $H_{\text{Hardware}} = 1/N$ when all actors have equal shares.
3. Monopoly: $H_{\text{Hardware}} = 1$ when a single actor controls all compute.

B.2 Model Weights Concentration Index

H_{\text{Weights}}(t) = \sum_{j=1}^{M} w_j^2(t)

where $w_j(t)$ is the share of strategically relevant model weights (measured in effective parameters weighted by capability) controlled by actor $j$ at time $t$.

Distinction from Hardware: $H_{\text{Weights}}$ captures concentration in open vs. closed model weights and foundational datasets, which may diverge from hardware concentration due to:

· Open-source model releases (reduces $H_{\text{Weights}}$ without affecting $H_{\text{Hardware}}$)
· Proprietary model development (increases $H_{\text{Weights}}$ without increasing $H_{\text{Hardware}}$)
· Model distillation and compression (reduces hardware requirements while maintaining weight dependency)

B.3 Combined Concentration Measure

DCCR(t) = \left( H_{\text{Hardware}}(t)^{\alpha} + H_{\text{Weights}}(t)^{\beta} \right) \cdot D_P(t)^{\gamma_2} \cdot C_I(t)^{\gamma_3} \cdot A_D(t)^{\gamma_4}

where:

· $\alpha, \beta > 0$ are concentration sensitivity parameters
· $\gamma_2, \gamma_3, \gamma_4 > 0$ are dependency, criticality, and delegation sensitivity parameters
· $D_P(t) \in [0,1]$ = dependency on private infrastructure
· $C_I(t) \in [0,1]$ = criticality of affected infrastructure
· $A_D(t) \in [0,1]$ = degree of delegated decision authority

Key Property (Zero Delegation): If $A_D = 0$, then $DCCR = 0$ regardless of concentration levels. This formalizes the insight that compute concentration alone is not a sovereign threat; it becomes a threat only when political authority is delegated to concentrated infrastructure.

Key Property (Zero Concentration): If $H_{\text{Hardware}} = 0$ and $H_{\text{Weights}} = 0$ (perfectly competitive markets), then $DCCR = 0$ regardless of delegation. This formalizes the intuition that competitive markets reduce sovereign risk.

B.4 Temporal Dynamics

The dynamics of DCCR depend on the interaction of technological and institutional factors:

\frac{\mathrm{d}DCCR}{\mathrm{d}t} = \frac{\partial DCCR}{\partial H_{\text{Hardware}}} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{d}H_{\text{Hardware}}}{\mathrm{d}t} + \frac{\partial DCCR}{\partial H_{\text{Weights}}} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{d}H_{\text{Weights}}}{\mathrm{d}t} + \frac{\partial DCCR}{\partial D_P} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{d}D_P}{\mathrm{d}t} + \frac{\partial DCCR}{\partial A_D} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{d}A_D}{\mathrm{d}t}

Technological drivers:

· $\frac{\mathrm{d}H_{\text{Hardware}}}{\mathrm{d}t}$: determined by capital investment patterns, economies of scale, and regulatory interventions
· $\frac{\mathrm{d}H_{\text{Weights}}}{\mathrm{d}t}$: determined by open-source vs. proprietary development strategies

Institutional drivers:

· $\frac{\mathrm{d}D_P}{\mathrm{d}t}$: determined by procurement decisions, sovereignty policies
· $\frac{\mathrm{d}A_D}{\mathrm{d}t}$: determined by governance frameworks, oversight capacity

