Appendix C: The Human Agency Preservation Index—Theoretical Foundations

C.1 Formal Definition

HAI = A \times C \times P \times R

where:

· $A \in [0,1]$ = individual agency (capacity to act autonomously)
· $C \in [0,1]$ = contestability (capacity to challenge decisions)
· $P \in [0,1]$ = participation (capacity to influence collective decisions)
· $R \in [0,1]$ = reversibility (capacity to undo decisions)

C.2 Axiomatic Foundation

Axiom H1 (Non-Negativity): $HAI \geq 0$ for all systems.

Axiom H2 (Normalization): $HAI = 1$ if and only if $A = C = P = R = 1$.

Axiom H3 (Non-Substitutability): If any dimension equals zero, then $HAI = 0$, regardless of the values of other dimensions.

Axiom H4 (Monotonicity): $HAI$ is strictly increasing in each dimension when all dimensions are positive.

Axiom H5 (Symmetry): All dimensions are equally weighted in the multiplicative form.

Proposition C.1 (Uniqueness of Multiplicative Form). The multiplicative form $HAI = A \times C \times P \times R$ is the unique (up to monotonic transformation) aggregation function satisfying Axioms H1–H5.

Proof.

Step 1: Axiom H3 (non-substitutability) requires that $HAI = 0$ whenever any dimension equals zero. This rules out additive forms:

HAI_{\text{additive}} = \frac{A + C + P + R}{4}

which would give $HAI_{\text{additive}} = 0.75$ when $A = C = P = 1$ and $R = 0$, violating non-substitutability.

Step 2: Axiom H4 (monotonicity) requires that $HAI$ be strictly increasing in each dimension. This rules out threshold-based forms:

HAI_{\text{threshold}} = \min\{A, C, P, R\}

which, while satisfying non-substitutability, is not strictly increasing (increasing a non-minimal dimension does not increase $HAI$).

Step 3: Axiom H5 (symmetry) requires that all dimensions be treated identically. Combined with H3 and H4, this implies that the aggregation function must be a symmetric function that is zero whenever any argument is zero and strictly increasing otherwise.

Step 4: The only continuous functions satisfying these properties are of the form:

HAI = f(A \times C \times P \times R)

where $f$ is a strictly increasing function with $f(0) = 0$ and $f(1) = 1$.

Step 5: Axiom H2 (normalization) selects $f(x) = x$, yielding the unique multiplicative form. $\blacksquare$

C.3 Relationship to AI Success

Definition C.2 (AI Success). AI Success is defined as:

\text{AI Success} = \text{Capability} \times \text{Human Agency}

where Capability $\geq 0$ is a measure of technical performance, and Human Agency $= HAI \in [0,1]$.

Proposition C.3 (Agency Collapse). If $HAI = 0$, then AI Success $= 0$, regardless of Capability.

Proof. Immediate from the definition. $\square$

Implication: A highly capable AI system that destroys human agency has zero social value. This operationalizes the principle of Human-Machine Complementarity.

C.4 Dimensional Specification

A (Individual Agency): Measured through:

· Decision autonomy (can individuals make choices without algorithmic mediation?)
· Information access (can individuals access information needed for autonomous decision-making?)
· Cognitive load (does the system reduce or increase cognitive burden?)

C (Contestability): Measured through:

· Appeal mechanisms (can decisions be challenged?)
· Review accessibility (is review available to all affected parties?)
· Reversal rates (how often are algorithmic decisions overturned?)

P (Participation): Measured through:

· Deliberative inclusion (are affected communities consulted?)
· Co-design processes (do citizens participate in system design?)
· Representative oversight (are democratic institutions involved in governance?)

R (Reversibility): Measured through:

· Temporal reversibility (can decisions be undone?)
· Scope of reversibility (are all effects reversible?)
· Cost of reversibility (economic, social, political costs of reversal?)

