Appendix F: Hybrid Public Utility Model—Formal Specification

F.1 Utility Regulation Framework

Definition F.1 (Compute Utility). A compute utility is an entity that provides computational infrastructure or services essential for public administration and is subject to regulatory oversight to ensure non-discriminatory access, reasonable pricing, and service reliability.

Regulatory Instruments:

1. Access Mandates: Require utilities to provide access to all qualified public institutions at regulated rates.
2. Interoperability Requirements: Require utilities to support open standards for API access.
3. Structural Separation: Prohibit utilities from owning applications that make decisions affecting fundamental rights.
4. Capacity Reservations: Require utilities to reserve a percentage of capacity for public research and civil society.
5. Sovereign Reserve Margins: Require utilities to maintain emergency capacity under public control.

F.2 Ostrom Governance Layers

Layer 1: Local (Municipal)

· Local compute cooperatives for community needs
· Local oversight of public sector AI deployments
· Community participation in algorithmic governance

Layer 2: National (State)

· National compute commons with mandatory capacity reservations
· National AI audit office with technical expertise
· Parliamentary oversight of compute allocation

Layer 3: Regional (EU/Continental)

· Regional interoperability standards
· Cross-border compute sharing agreements
· Regional AI governance frameworks

Layer 4: International (Global)

· International compute access agreements
· Global AI governance coordination
· Technology transfer mechanisms

F.3 Capital Reinvestment Model

Definition F.2 (Compute Depreciation). Compute infrastructure depreciates at rate $\delta \in (0,1)$ per annum, reflecting hardware obsolescence (3-5 year cycles), energy efficiency improvements, and security updates.

Investment Requirement: To maintain constant compute capacity $C_A$:

I_{\text{required}} = \delta \cdot C_A

Underinvestment Risk: If actual investment $I < I_{\text{required}}$, compute capacity declines:

\frac{\mathrm{d}C_A}{\mathrm{d}t} = I - \delta C_A

Sovereign Implication: States that rely entirely on private investment without public capital reserves face underinvestment risk during economic downturns, when private capital withdraws from compute infrastructure.

F.4 Energy Grid Dependency

Compute infrastructure requires stable power supply. The energy intensity of hyperscale data centers (100+ MW) creates grid dependency:

Grid Constraint: $P_{\text{compute}} + P_{\text{other}} \leq P_{\text{grid,max}}$

where $P_{\text{compute}}$ is compute power demand, $P_{\text{other}}$ is other demand, and $P_{\text{grid,max}}$ is grid capacity.

Sovereign Implication: States with limited grid capacity face hard constraints on domestic compute expansion, increasing dependency on foreign cloud providers.

F.5 Fab Dependency

Semiconductor manufacturing is concentrated in a few jurisdictions:

Current Concentration: Approximately 90% of advanced semiconductor manufacturing capacity is located in Taiwan and South Korea.

Sovereign Risk: Disruption of semiconductor supply chains (geopolitical, natural disaster, pandemic) creates critical vulnerability for compute-dependent states.

Mitigation: Sovereign compute reserve margins must account for fab dependency by maintaining diverse supply chains and strategic stockpiles.

